# specifications

Path: `documentation/specifications/`. The repository has no root README. The structure is in the playbook.

| Topic | SSOT |
| ----- | ---- |
| Tree of directories, files of a service directory, `readme.md` or `index.md`, branch rules | `playbook/workflow/specifications-structure.md` |
| Why the contract is here and not in the code of a service | `playbook/principles/contract-first.md`, `playbook/principles/spec-first.md` |
| Content of Kafka message schemas, required headers, consumer and producer code | `documentation/specifications/messaging/README.md` |
| Common message components | `documentation/specifications/messaging/common-message.yaml` |
| Common REST schemas | `documentation/specifications/microservices/common/` |
| Platform decisions for REST, database, Kafka, codegen | rule `adr-topics` |
| Notation of fields, types, and enum values | `playbook/principles/naming-and-notation.md` |
| Owners and review | `playbook/workflow/generic-git-flow.md` (SA; AR must review) |

Before you write a file, do these checks:

1. Put message schemas only in `messaging/`. In a service directory, put a `$ref` to them.
2. Use one `openapi.yaml` for each service.
3. Do not copy platform REST conventions into a service specification. Put a link to the ADR.
4. Write the specification as "how it must work in production". The `master` branch is the source of truth.
5. Do not put links to code, schemas in `data-schemas/`, or Jira. These are lower layers.
